  "options": {
    "A": "G must be isomorphic to S₄.",
    "B": "The kernel of the conjugation action has order 1 or 2, and its image is respectively isomorphic to S₄ or A₄.",
    "C": "The kernel of the conjugation action has order divisible by 4.",
    "D": "G must possess a normal Sylow 2-subgroup."
  },
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": "Let P be a Sylow 3-subgroup and let K be the kernel of the conjugation action. Since the orbit of P has size 4, the orbit-stabilizer theorem gives |N_G(P)| = 24/4 = 6. Because every element of K normalizes P, K is a subgroup of N_G(P), so |K| divides 6. The possibilities 3 and 6 are impossible: in either case K would contain a unique Sylow 3-subgroup that is characteristic in K and hence normal in G, contradicting the existence of four Sylow 3-subgroups. Thus |K| is 1 or 2. Consequently, the image has order 24 or 12. A subgroup of S₄ of order 24 is S₄ itself, while a subgroup of order 12 is the unique index-two subgroup A₄. Notice that G need not be isomorphic to S₄: A₄ × C₂ also has four Sylow 3-subgroups and gives the A₄ image case."
}

