  "question": "A teacher wants students to demonstrate high-level cognitive learning by developing an original solution to a community problem. Which objective is most appropriately aligned with Bloom's Taxonomy?",
  "options": {
    "A": "Students will remember the major causes of the community problem.",
    "B": "Students will understand why the community problem occurs.",
    "C": "Students will apply a previously taught solution to the community problem.",
    "D": "Students will create an original solution to the community problem using the available resources."
  },
  "correct_answer": "D",
  "explanation": "Creating an original solution reflects the highest level of Bloom's revised taxonomy because it requires students to integrate knowledge and skills to produce a novel outcome. Options A, B, and C represent progressively higher levels but remain below the create level."
}

{
  "question": "A learner can complete about 60% of a new type of problem independently but reaches 85% with strategic prompts. Which instructional approach best reflects Vygotsky’s Zone of Proximal Development?",
  "options": {
    "A": "Wait until the learner can solve every problem independently before providing instruction.",
    "B": "Demonstrate the entire procedure once and then remove all opportunities for learner practice.",
    "C": "Provide permanent, full-step assistance so the learner does not encounter task difficulty.",
    "D": "Offer targeted hints and worked examples, then gradually withdraw them as learner competence increases."
  },
  "correct_answer": "D",
  "explanation": "The ZPD is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can accomplish with appropriate guidance. Providing contingent scaffolds within this gap, followed by gradual fading, promotes internalized learning and independent mastery."
}

{
  "question": "A teacher introduces a complex problem, guides students through it with graduated support, and organizes small-group discussions so that peers can explain and challenge one another's reasoning. Which learning theory most directly informs this approach?",
  "options": {
    "A": "Behaviorism",
    "B": "Social constructivism",
    "C": "Nativism",
    "D": "Classical conditioning"
  },
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": "Social constructivism, associated with Lev Vygotsky, emphasizes that learners construct knowledge through social interaction, language, and guidance from more capable others within the zone of proximal development."
}

