}

{
  "question": "After introducing weekly low-stakes quizzes with item-level feedback and peer explanation, a course finds that students perform better on later assessments and report more frequent self-regulated study. Which interpretation is most consistent with formative assessment theory and research on its mechanisms?",
  "options": {
    "A": "The improvement is necessarily caused by the quizzes because all students completed them, even without a comparison group.",
    "B": "Formative assessment provides repeated opportunities for retrieval, explanatory feedback, and evaluation of understanding, enabling students and instructors to adjust learning before high-stakes assessment; increased engagement is a plausible mediated outcome.",
    "C": "Its primary effect is motivational and results mainly from awarding additional points for completing the quizzes.",
    "D": "Because the assessments are low-stakes, they cannot substantially influence learning or engagement."
  },
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": "Formative assessment is effective because it makes students' current understanding visible and provides timely feedback and opportunities for self-assessment, peer interaction, and revision. These processes support conceptual understanding, self-regulated learning, targeted instructional adjustment, and sustained engagement. The other options either overstate causal certainty, misidentify the main mechanism, or incorrectly assume that low-stakes assessments have no learning value."
}

{
  "question": "A learner can solve routine problems independently but requires targeted prompts to solve a more complex, unfamiliar problem. After succeeding with the prompts, the learner begins to complete similar tasks with decreasing assistance. Which approach most faithfully reflects Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development?",
  "options": {
    "A": "Postpone instruction until the learner can complete the complex problem entirely without assistance.",
    "B": "Provide calibrated scaffolding within the learner's emerging capabilities and gradually withdraw it as development progresses.",
    "C": "Administer the same unsupported assessment repeatedly to determine whether performance has stabilized.",
    "D": "Replace the complex task with an easier task that the learner can already complete independently."
