{
  "Question": "Two ligands bind the same receptor through simple 1:1 equilibrium binding. Ligand X has a dissociation constant (Kd) of 1 nM and produces the same maximum response as a full agonist. Ligand Y has a Kd of 10 nM and a maximum response equal to 50% of the full agonist. Which statement is most accurate?",
  "Options": {
    "A": "Ligand X has higher affinity, but the difference in maximum response cannot be attributed to intrinsic activity or efficacy.",
    "B": "Ligand X has 10-fold higher affinity, whereas ligand Y has lower intrinsic efficacy; at saturating concentrations, receptor occupancy may approach 100% for both despite different maximum responses.",
    "C": "Ligand Y has higher affinity because its Kd is greater, and affinity directly determines intrinsic activity.",
    "D": "Ligand X must produce a greater maximum response because lower Kd always corresponds to greater intrinsic efficacy."
  },
  "Correct_Answer": "B",
  "Explanation": "Affinity is inversely related to Kd, so X has 10-fold higher affinity than Y. Affinity describes receptor occupancy, whereas efficacy describes the ability of a bound ligand to activate the receptor. At sufficiently high concentrations, both ligands may occupy nearly all receptors, but Y produces less receptor activation and therefore has lower intrinsic efficacy, consistent with partial-agonist behavior."
}

