    "B": "Ritonavir irreversibly binds tacrolimus and increases its free concentration by displacing it from plasma proteins.",
    "C": "Ritonavir inhibits CYP3A4/5 and P-glycoprotein, reducing tacrolimus clearance and increasing exposure; the prolonged recovery is related to tacrolimus's long effective half-life and concentration-dependent toxicity.",
    "D": "Tacrolimus is primarily eliminated by CYP2D6, so ritonavir has no effect on its metabolism; the renal injury is caused by a pharmacodynamic interaction with mycophenolate.",
    "E": "The increased tacrolimus concentration results from accelerated gastric emptying, and persistence after drug withdrawal indicates tachyphylaxis of renal transporters."
  },
  "correct_answer": "C",
  "explanation": "Tacrolimus is a CYP3A4/5 and P-glycoprotein substrate with a narrow therapeutic index. Ritonavir strongly inhibits these pathways, markedly increasing tacrolius exposure and causing concentration-dependent nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity. The elevated concentration may persist after withdrawal because inhibition wanes gradually and tacrolimus has a long effective half-life. Therapeutic drug monitoring and substantial dose reduction or temporary discontinuation are generally required."
}

{
  "question": "A patient with culture-proven bacterial sepsis develops refractory hypotension, pulmonary edema, thrombocytopenia, elevated D-dimer, and worsening renal function despite appropriate antimicrobials and fluid resuscitation. Early measurements show markedly elevated IL-6 and TNF-α, followed later by reduced HLA-DR expression and impaired T-cell proliferation. Which statement best integrates the pathophysiology and management of sepsis-associated cytokine dysregulation?",
  "options": [
