      "label": "A",
      "text": "The dominant abnormality is isolated excess of pro-inflammatory cytokines; therefore, routine blockade of IL-6 or TNF-α should be initiated in every patient with sepsis."
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "Sepsis involves dynamic and tissue-specific dysregulation in which early hyperinflammation coexists with compensatory anti-inflammatory responses and later immunoparalysis; management prioritizes prompt antimicrobials, source control, hemodynamic and organ support, with corticosteroids considered in selected patients with ongoing vasopressor-dependent shock."
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "Cytokine storm is primarily a consequence of adaptive immune-cell activation, and immunosuppression is not clinically relevant in septic shock."
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "Once endothelial injury and microvascular thrombosis occur, immunomodulatory therapy can reverse the established multiorgan dysfunction reliably, making routine cytokine inhibition standard care."
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": "Sepsis is not characterized uniformly by either cytokine excess or immunosuppression. Innate immune activation may produce TNF-α, IL-1, IL-6, endothelial dysfunction, vascular leak, and coagulopathy, while concurrent or subsequent anti-inflammatory mechanisms produce immunoparalysis. Accordingly, definitive treatment centers on early appropriate antimicrobials, source control, resuscitation, and organ support; targeted immunomodulation is reserved for selected indications, such as corticosteroid use in ongoing vasopressor-dependent septic shock, rather than routine cytokine blockade."
}

