    "key_points": [
      {
        "concept": "Acids and bases in water",
        "explanation": "In water, an acid increases the concentration of H3O+ ions, while a base reduces H3O+ concentration by producing OH- ions or accepting protons."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Brønsted-Lowry definition",
        "explanation": "An acid donates a proton, H+, and a base accepts a proton. This definition explains neutralization reactions beyond substances that contain hydrogen or hydroxide ions."
      },
      {
        "concept": "The pH scale",
        "explanation": "For ideal dilute aqueous solutions, pH = -log10[a(H3O+)]. At approximately 25 °C, pH 7 is neutral, pH values below 7 indicate acidic solutions, and values above 7 indicate basic solutions."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Logarithmic nature of pH",
        "explanation": "Each one-unit change in pH corresponds to a tenfold change in hydronium ion concentration. A solution with pH 3 has approximately ten times the hydronium ion concentration of a solution with pH 4."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Indicators",
        "explanation": "Acid-base indicators change color at characteristic pH ranges. Red cabbage juice can provide a broad estimate, while calibrated pH paper or a pH meter provides more precise measurements."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Strength versus concentration",
        "explanation": "A strong acid ionizes almost completely, whereas a weak acid ionizes only partially. Strong does not mean concentrated, and dilute does not mean weak."
