        "theorem": "Green's theorem, circulation form",
        "statement": "If C is the positively oriented boundary of a planar region D and F = (P,Q) has continuous first partial derivatives near D, then ∮_C F · dr = ∮_C (P dx + Q dy) = ∬_D (Q_x − P_y) dA.",
        "orientation": "For a simple region, counterclockwise traversal gives the outward-normal convention used in the flux form and the positive orientation in the circulation form.",
        "interpretation": "The circulation around the boundary is determined by the local scalar curl Q_x − P_y inside the region.",
        "typical_use": "Evaluating a closed planar line integral more easily than direct parameterization, or checking a direct calculation."
      },
      {
        "theorem": "Green's theorem, flux form",
        "statement": "For a positively oriented boundary C of D, ∮_C F · n ds = ∮_C (P dy − Q dx) = ∬_D (P_x + Q_y) dA.",
        "orientation": "The normal n is outward from D.",
        "interpretation": "The total flux across the boundary is determined by the local divergence inside the region.",
        "typical_use": "Converting planar flux integrals into double integrals."
      },
      {
        "theorem": "Divergence theorem",
        "statement": "If V is a bounded solid with piecewise smooth closed boundary ∂V and F has continuous first partial derivatives near V, then ∯_∂V F · n dS = ∭_V div F dV.",
        "orientation": "The normal on ∂V is outward.",
        "interpretation": "The net flux through a closed surface equals the total source or sink strength inside the solid.",
        "typical_use": "Evaluating difficult surface integrals by replacing them with volume integrals."
      },
      {
        "theorem": "Stokes' theorem",
        "statement": "If S is an oriented piecewise smooth surface with boundary C and F has continuous first partial derivatives on an open neighborhood of S, then ∫_C F · dr = ∬_S (curl F) · n dS.",
        "orientation": "The direction of C is induced by n through the right-hand rule: point the thumb along n and let the fingers indicate the positive direction around C.",
        "interpretation": "The circulation around the boundary is determined by the normal component of curl over the spanning surface.",
        "typical_use": "Evaluating circulation around a space curve, especially when a simple spanning surface is easier to parameterize than the curve is to integrate directly."
      },
