    "key_points": [
      {
        "point": "Measure spaces",
        "explanation": "A measure space consists of a set X, a sigma-algebra 𝓜 of subsets of X, and a measure μ defined on 𝓜. Lebesgue measure is the standard measure that assigns interval length to intervals."
      },
      {
        "point": "Sigma-algebra closure",
        "explanation": "A sigma-algebra is closed under complements and countable unions. This closure requirement makes it suitable for describing measurable events and supports the countable additivity of measures."
      },
      {
        "point": "Null sets and almost-everywhere equality",
        "explanation": "A set N is null if μ(N)=0. Statements that hold outside a null set are said to hold almost everywhere, or a.e. Lebesgue integrals are unchanged by modifying a measurable function on a null set."
      },
      {
        "point": "Measurable functions",
        "explanation": "A real-valued function f is measurable if the inverse image of every suitable interval, equivalently the set {x : f(x)>a} for every real a, is measurable."
      },
      {
        "point": "Simple functions",
        "explanation": "A nonnegative simple function takes finitely many values and can be written as a finite sum s=Σ cᵢ1_Eᵢ, where Eᵢ are measurable. Its integral is Σ cᵢμ(Eᵢ)."
      },
      {
        "point": "Definition of the Lebesgue integral",
        "explanation": "For a nonnegative measurable function f, the integral is defined as the supremum of the integrals of all simple functions s satisfying 0≤s≤f."
      },
      {
        "point": "Positive and negative parts",
        "explanation": "For a general measurable function f, define f⁺=max(f,0) and f⁻=max(−f,0). Then ∫f is defined by ∫f⁺−∫f⁻ only when the two terms are not both infinite."
      },
      {
        "point": "Convergence theorems",
        "explanation": "The monotone convergence theorem handles increasing nonnegative sequences, Fatou's lemma gives an inequality for general nonnegative sequences, and the dominated convergence theorem gives equality under an integrable dominating function."
      },
      {
        "point": "Product integration",
        "explanation": "Tonelli's theorem permits exchanging iterated integrals for nonnegative measurable functions. Fubini's theorem does the same for integrable functions."
      },
      {
        "point": "Riemann versus Lebesgue integration",
        "explanation": "Every Riemann-integrable function on a compact interval is Lebesgue integrable with the same value, but the converse is false. The function 1_Q on [0,1] is a standard counterexample."
      }
