  "key_points": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "title": "Entanglement is a property of the composite state",
      "explanation": "A pure bipartite state is a product state if it can be written as |ψ⟩ = |α⟩_A ⊗ |β⟩_B. A pure state is entangled if no such factorization exists. A mixed state is separable if it can be written as a convex sum of product states; otherwise it is entangled."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "title": "Superposition and correlation do not automatically imply entanglement",
      "explanation": "The state (|00⟩ + |01⟩)/√2 is a product state because it equals |0⟩_A ⊗ (|0⟩_B + |1⟩_B)/√2. It nevertheless produces correlated computational-basis outcomes. Therefore, correlation alone is not a sufficient test for entanglement."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "title": "Bell states display strong nonclassical correlations",
      "explanation": "For |Φ+⟩ = (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2, computational-basis outcomes are perfectly correlated, while each local outcome is individually random. For the singlet state |Ψ−⟩ = (|01⟩ − |10⟩)/√2, measurements along the same axis are perfectly anticorrelated."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "title": "Bell non-locality is defined operationally",
      "explanation": "Bell non-locality means that observed correlations cannot be reproduced by a local hidden-variable model satisfying local causality and measurement independence. More precisely, Bell tests exclude factorizable models of the form P(a,b|x,y) = P(a|x)P(b|y), together with an appropriate distribution over hidden variables."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "title": "The CHSH inequality distinguishes local models from quantum predictions",
      "explanation": "Any local hidden-variable model satisfies |S| ≤ 2. Quantum mechanics permits values up to 2√2, known as the Tsirelson bound. A statistically reliable experimental value greater than 2 violates CHSH and rules out the corresponding class of local models."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "title": "Non-local correlations cannot be used for controllable faster-than-light signaling",
      "explanation": "Alice’s choice of measurement does not change Bob’s unconditioned local statistics. For a maximally entangled state, Bob’s reduced state is maximally mixed, for example ρ_B = I/2. Bob cannot infer Alice’s measurement setting or result unless Alice sends classical information."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7",
      "title": "Entanglement and Bell non-locality are related but not identical for mixed states",
      "explanation": "Under standard definitions, Bell-nonlocal states form a subset of steerable states, which form a subset of entangled states. Noisy mixed states can be entangled while still admitting a local model for a particular experiment, or even for all CHSH tests."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8",
      "title": "Bell experiments do not by themselves settle all interpretations of quantum mechanics",
      "explanation": "Copenhagen, many-worlds, relational, and other interpretations use the same empirical Bell predictions. Bell experiments establish the failure of local hidden-variable explanations under their assumptions, but they do not uniquely select one philosophical interpretation."
