      "content": [
        "Many quantum-chemical Hamiltonians are too complicated to solve exactly, even though they may consist of a familiar main Hamiltonian plus a weak correction.",
        "Examples include a weak external electric or magnetic field, a small relativistic correction, weak spin–orbit coupling, or a small interaction between nearly stationary states.",
        "Perturbation theory converts the difficult exact problem into a sequence of corrections to a solvable reference problem."
      ],
      "teacher_guidance": "Begin with a familiar system such as a harmonic oscillator or an infinite square well, then ask how the spectrum would change if a weak additional potential were introduced."
