    "core_content_outline": [
      {
        "section": "1. Foundations of genetic engineering",
        "core_ideas": [
          "Genes are specific DNA sequences that can influence traits or the production of functional molecules.",
          "A genome is the complete set of genetic material in an organism or cell.",
          "Genetic engineering deliberately changes genetic material to achieve a research, medical, agricultural, or industrial goal.",
          "A representative traditional workflow includes identifying or constructing DNA, inserting it into a vector, transferring it into a host, selecting modified cells, and verifying the result.",
          "Gene addition introduces new DNA, whereas gene editing modifies a particular existing genomic site."
        ]
      },
      {
        "section": "2. CRISPR-Cas9 mechanism",
        "core_ideas": [
          "The CRISPR-Cas system is a natural adaptive immune mechanism found in some bacteria and archaea.",
          "A guide RNA directs the Cas9-gRNA complex to a complementary DNA sequence.",
          "Cas9 recognizes a suitable PAM and makes a double-strand break in the DNA.",
          "The cell's DNA-repair machinery then processes the break.",
          "Non-homologous end joining may create small insertions or deletions that disrupt a gene.",
          "Homology-directed repair may use a supplied DNA template to introduce a desired sequence.",
          "The final outcome depends on the target, guide RNA, delivery method, cell type, and repair pathway."
        ]
      },
      {
        "section": "3. Applications",
        "core_ideas": [
          "Research: studying gene function and generating disease models.",
          "Medicine: investigating treatments for inherited disorders and some infectious diseases.",
          "Agriculture: improving disease resistance, product quality, or resilience in some crops.",
          "Diagnostics: using some CRISPR-associated systems to detect DNA or RNA sequences.",
          "Some applications are established or clinically used, while many remain experimental and require further validation."
        ]
      },
      {
        "section": "4. Limitations and risks",
        "core_ideas": [
          "Off-target editing can affect unintended DNA sites.",
          "Unwanted insertions, deletions, rearrangements, or repair errors may occur.",
          "Efficient delivery to the intended cells and tissues remains a major challenge.",
          "Immune responses and differences between cell types can affect safety and performance.",
          "An edit in one cell may not affect every cell in an organism, creating mosaicism.",
          "Cost, access, regulation, and public trust influence whether benefits are broadly shared."
        ]
      },
      {
        "section": "5. Ethics and governance",
        "core_ideas": [
          "Somatic editing affects non-reproductive cells and is generally not expected to be inherited.",
          "Germline editing affects eggs, sperm, or embryos and may be passed to future generations.",
          "Important questions include informed consent, fairness, privacy, disability rights, enhancement, environmental impact, animal welfare, and responsibility for long-term effects.",
          "Scientific evidence can inform ethical decisions, but ethical decisions also involve values, rights, and social priorities."
