      "Practice systematic debugging and collaborative problem-solving."
  ],
  "key_points": [
    {
      "concept": "Meaning of recursion",
      "explanation": "Recursion occurs when a function calls itself either directly or indirectly to solve a problem."
    },
    {
      "concept": "Two essential components",
      "explanation": "Every correct recursive solution needs a base case that stops the recursion and a recursive case that moves toward the base case."
    },
    {
      "concept": "Problem decomposition",
      "explanation": "A problem is divided into smaller instances of the same type of problem until a directly solvable case is reached."
    },
    {
      "concept": "Call stack",
      "explanation": "The call stack stores active function calls, including parameters, local variables, and the return point. It grows when a function calls another function and shrinks as calls return."
    },
    {
      "concept": "Execution and unwinding",
      "explanation": "Recursive calls move toward the base case. After the base case returns a value, the pending calls resume and may combine that value into the final result."
    },
    {
      "concept": "Recursion and iteration",
      "explanation": "Many problems can be solved with either recursion or iteration. Recursion can be concise and natural for hierarchical or self-similar problems, while iteration is often easier to trace and may use less memory."
    }
  ],
  "common_difficulties": [
