      {
        "topic": "Asymptotic notation and tight bounds",
        "core_idea": "O(f(n)) gives an asymptotic upper bound, Omega(f(n)) gives an asymptotic lower bound, and Theta(f(n)) requires matching upper and lower bounds.",
        "definitions": [
          "T(n) = O(f(n)) if constants c and n0 exist such that 0 ≤ T(n) ≤ cf(n) for n ≥ n0.",
          "T(n) = Omega(f(n)) if constants c > 0 and n0 exist such that T(n) ≥ cf(n) for n ≥ n0.",
          "T(n) = o(f(n)) if the limit of T(n)/f(n) is zero.",
