      "condition_variable_pattern": [
        "acquire(buffer_lock)",
        "while buffer_count equals buffer_capacity, wait on notFull",
        "put an item and update the shared predicate",
        "signal notEmpty",
        "release(buffer_lock)"
      ],
      "invariant": "The number of items in the buffer must remain between 0 and buffer_capacity, and every stored item must be consumed according to the specified queue or buffer discipline.",
      "takeaway": "A lock protects the data structure; semaphores or condition variables express availability and state transitions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "S6",
      "title": "Deadlock, livelock, and starvation",
      "teacher_explanation": [
        "Deadlock requires mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, and circular wait.",
        "Prevention removes at least one condition. Avoidance selects a resource-allocation decision that preserves a safe state. Detection and recovery allow a system to detect a cycle and resolve it after it occurs.",
        "A simple global lock-ordering rule is often sufficient: every thread that needs multiple locks must request them in the same order.",
        "Deadlock means no participant can proceed. Livelock means participants are active but make no useful progress. Starvation means one participant is indefinitely denied while others continue."
      ],
      "discussion_prompts": [
        "Does a lock guarantee that a system cannot deadlock?",
        "What is the difference between a cycle in a resource-allocation graph and ordinary contention?",
        "Can a scheduler that gives priority to one class of threads cause starvation for another class?"
      ],
      "takeaway": "A correct concurrent design must consider both safety and liveness."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "S7",
      "title": "Performance, fairness, and memory consistency",
      "teacher_explanation": [
        "Fine-grained locks can increase concurrency but may increase overhead and expose ordering problems. A single global lock is simple but can serialize all workers.",
        "Context switches, cache-coherence traffic, false sharing, lock contention, and blocking operations all affect scalability.",
        "Priority inversion occurs when a low-priority lock holder blocks a high-priority thread while a medium-priority thread runs. Priority inheritance can mitigate this problem.",
        "Memory visibility is distinct from mutual exclusion. A lock may establish ordering, but a programmer must use it consistently. Atomic operations and acquire-release semantics may be required in language-specific code.",
        "The volatile keyword may control some compiler optimizations, but it does not generally provide inter-thread atomicity or the required ordering."
      ],
      "takeaway": "The fastest synchronization design is not necessarily the clearest or safest design; correctness, fairness, and observability must be evaluated together."
    }
  ],
  "synchronization_primitive_comparison": [
    {
      "primitive": "Atomic operation",
      "semantics": "Performs one indivisible operation, such as an atomic increment, test-and-set, or compare-and-swap",
      "typical_use": "Simple counters, flags, and lock implementation",
      "common_pitfall": "Assuming that several individually atomic operations together preserve a compound invariant"
    },
    {
      "primitive": "Mutex",
      "semantics": "Provides exclusive access; the execution context that owns the mutex is normally responsible for releasing it",
      "typical_use": "Protecting a short critical section or an object invariant",
      "common_pitfall": "Holding it during blocking I/O, using inconsistent lock order, or protecting an unnecessarily large region"
    },
    {
      "primitive": "Binary semaphore",
      "semantics": "A semaphore with permits taking values 0 or 1; wait consumes a permit and signal adds one",
      "typical_use": "Simple mutual exclusion or event gating",
      "common_pitfall": "Confusing a permit with an ownership rule or using a signal as a substitute for checking a predicate"
    },
    {
      "primitive": "Counting semaphore",
      "semantics": "Maintains a count of available permits",
      "typical_use": "Bounding resources and implementing producer-consumer coordination",
      "common_pitfall": "Incorrect ordering of wait and signal operations, or forgetting to protect the protected data structure separately"
    },
    {
      "primitive": "Condition variable",
      "semantics": "Allows a thread to wait while an associated mutex is held; the mutex is released during the wait",
      "typical_use": "Waiting until a queue is nonempty, a buffer is not full, or a state predicate changes",
      "common_pitfall": "Using if instead of while, signaling without updating the predicate, or assuming that signal immediately transfers execution"
    },
    {
      "primitive": "Monitor",
      "semantics": "Encapsulates shared data, a lock, and condition variables behind a controlled interface",
      "typical_use": "Structured object-oriented concurrent design",
      "common_pitfall": "Assuming encapsulation automatically eliminates design errors in the monitor's internal operations"
    },
    {
      "primitive": "Read-write lock",
      "semantics": "Allows multiple simultaneous readers or one writer, depending on the implementation and policy",
      "typical_use": "Read-heavy shared data",
      "common_pitfall": "Writer starvation, reader starvation, and unnecessary complexity"
    }
  ],
