        "question": "Explain why a QPE circuit uses controlled powers U^(2ʲ).",
        "expected_answer": "The powers create distinct relative phases corresponding to successive phase bits; phase kickback encodes φ on the ancillas, and the inverse QFT makes those bits more likely to be measured."
      },
      {
        "question": "Compare Grover and Shor in terms of speedup and problem structure.",
        "expected_answer": "Grover gives a quadratic improvement for unstructured black-box search. Shor gives a theoretical polynomial-time improvement for integer factoring through period or order finding, with a larger resource and fault-tolerance burden."
      },
      {
        "question": "A student claims that HHL will always outperform classical linear solvers. Identify three problems with the claim.",
        "expected_answer": "The claim ignores QRAM-style input access, state preparation, condition-number dependence, precision, amplitude-encoded output, and error-correction or runtime costs."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why can a variational quantum algorithm fail to deliver a speedup?",
        "expected_answer": "It may require many noisy measurements, suffer from optimization barriers or barren plateaus, have a weak classical competitor, or fail to outperform classical simulation at the relevant scale."
      }
    ],
    "exit_ticket_prompts": [
      "Name one algorithm that exploits periodicity.",
      "State the query-complexity advantage of Grover.",
      "Name one hidden assumption in Shor's practical implementation.",
      "Explain why superposition alone is not a source of speedup."
