      },
      {
        "topic": "PLC architecture",
        "content": "The power supply supports the CPU and modules; input modules receive signals from switches and sensors; the CPU executes user programs; output modules control lamps, relays, solenoid valves, or contactor coils; memory stores programs and data; communication interfaces connect the PLC to HMIs, industrial networks, and supervisory systems."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Input and output signals",
        "content": "Digital inputs represent on/off conditions, while digital outputs operate binary devices. Analog modules may be used for temperature, pressure, flow, and other continuously varying measurements."
      },
      {
        "topic": "PLC scan cycle",
        "content": "A typical PLC first reads the input image, executes the program from top to bottom, updates the output image, and then refreshes physical outputs. The order of instructions can affect the result."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Ladder logic",
        "content": "Horizontal lines represent electrical power flow, vertical lines represent instruction order, and left-side instructions read input or memory states. Normally open contacts are true when the associated bit is 1; normally closed contacts are true when the bit is 0."
      },
      {
