    "Content_Outline": [
      {
        "Section": 1. Review of Pure-Strategy Nash Equilibrium",
        "Key_Ideas": [
          "A best response maximizes a player's payoff given the opponent's strategy.",
          "A Nash equilibrium is a strategy profile in which every player's strategy is a best response.",
          "At equilibrium, no player can benefit from changing strategy alone."
        ]
      },
      {
        "Section": 2. Introduction to Mixed Strategies",
        "Key_Ideas": [
          "A mixed strategy assigns probabilities to available pure strategies.",
          "A pure strategy is a special mixed strategy that assigns probability one to one action.",
          "Mixed strategies are especially relevant when there is no pure best response or when the opponent is uncertain."
        ]
      },
      {
        "Section": 3. Expected Payoffs",
        "Key_Ideas": [
          "Expected payoff is a probability-weighted average of payoffs.",
          "Expected payoffs must be calculated separately for each player.",
          "A best response to a mixed strategy may be a pure action or another mixed strategy."
        ]
      },
      {
        "Section": 4. Mixed-Strategy Nash Equilibrium",
        "Key_Ideas": [
          "At a genuinely mixed equilibrium, at least one player assigns positive probability to multiple actions.",
          "Every action in a player's equilibrium support must give the same maximum expected payoff.",
          "An action excluded from the support cannot provide a strictly higher payoff."
        ]
      },
      {
        "Section": 5. Solving a Two-by-Two Game",
        "Key_Ideas": [
          "Let one player choose the first action with probability p and the other with probability q.",
          "To find one player's probability, make that player indifferent between the actions in the support.",
          "Solve the two indifference equations and then verify all deviations."
        ]
      },
      {
        "Section": 6. Existence and Interpretation",
        "Key_Ideas": [
          "Every finite game has at least one Nash equilibrium when pure and mixed strategies are allowed.",
          "The equilibrium may be pure, genuinely mixed, or multiple.",
          "Randomization at equilibrium creates unpredictability but removes profitable unilateral deviations."
