    {
      "point": "Market equilibrium",
      "explanation": "Equilibrium occurs at the intersection of demand and supply, where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. The corresponding values are the equilibrium price and equilibrium quantity."
    },
    {
      "point": "Disequilibrium",
      "explanation": "A price below equilibrium creates a shortage, which places upward pressure on price. A price above equilibrium creates excess supply, which places downward pressure on price."
    },
    {
      "point": "Dynamic adjustment",
      "explanation": "Shortages encourage buyers to bid prices up and attract more supply. Excess supply encourages sellers to reduce prices, which increases demand and reduces production."
    },
    {
      "point": "Changes in equilibrium",
      "explanation": "An increase in demand raises both equilibrium price and quantity. An increase in supply lowers equilibrium price and raises equilibrium quantity. When both curves shift, the quantity effect may be clear while the price effect can be ambiguous."
    },
    {
      "point": "Economic surplus",
      "explanation": "Consumer surplus is the difference between willingness to pay and the actual price. Producer surplus is the difference between the actual price and opportunity cost. Total surplus equals consumer surplus plus producer surplus."
    },
    {
      "point": "Allocative efficiency",
      "explanation": "A competitive equilibrium is allocatively efficient when total surplus is maximized because all units with willingness to pay greater than opportunity cost are traded and units with opportunity cost greater than willingness to pay are not produced."
    },
    {
      "point": "Efficiency versus equity",
      "explanation": "An efficient market does not automatically guarantee a fair distribution of income or surplus. Efficiency and equity are separate policy goals."
    }
