        "Point": "Ratio decidendi and obiter dicta",
        "Explanation": "The ratio decidendi is the legal principle, applied to material facts, that was necessary to the result. Obiter dicta are observations that were not necessary to the result. Obiter may be persuasive, and may later be adopted as part of another court's holding, but it is not binding merely because it appears in a judgment."
