        "point": "Stare decisis",
        "explanation": "The Latin phrase stare decisis means to stand by decided matters. It asks whether a court should adhere to a previously established legal principle despite possible disagreement with the earlier decision."
      },
      {
        "point": "Vertical precedent",
        "explanation": "A higher court normally binds lower courts within the same judicial hierarchy and jurisdiction. A trial court cannot simply disregard the controlling decision of an appellate court."
      },
      {
        "point": "Horizontal precedent",
        "explanation": "A court generally follows its own previous decisions, particularly when the same court or a court of equivalent authority has already determined the legal issue. The strength of this rule varies among jurisdictions."
      },
      {
        "point": "Binding versus persuasive authority",
        "explanation": "Binding authority is decisive when the court is within the precedent's hierarchy and the earlier case decided the same legal issue. Persuasive authority comes from another court, another jurisdiction, or a decision that is relevant but not controlling."
