        "example": "A fitness app may segment users, choose busy professionals as a target, and position itself as the most convenient solution for short, effective workouts."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Major segmentation bases",
        "explanation": "Common bases include demographic, geographic, psychographic, and behavioral variables. Effective analysis often combines several bases rather than relying on only one.",
        "example": "Age and income may describe reachability, while lifestyle and exercise behavior are often more useful for explaining preferences and needs."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Characteristics of a usable segment",
        "explanation": "A useful segment should be measurable or identifiable, accessible, substantial enough to serve, differentiable from other groups, and actionable. A group may be easy to describe but still be too small, inaccessible, or vague to serve profitably.",
        "example": "A group of highly interested customers may be attractive only if its size, willingness to pay, and accessibility can be estimated."
      },
      {
        "topic": "The segmentation process",
        "explanation": "A practical process includes defining the market and objective, gathering information, selecting bases, developing segment profiles, evaluating segments, selecting target groups, developing positioning, implementing the marketing mix, and monitoring results.",
