      "Appreciate the role of citizens, media, civil society and professional associations in democratic accountability."
    ]
  },
  "key_points": [
    {
      "point": "Meaning of bureaucratic accountability",
      "explanation": "Bureaucratic accountability is the obligation of public agencies and officials to explain decisions, demonstrate that authority has been used lawfully and in the public interest, respond to legitimate scrutiny, provide remedies where necessary, and learn from failures."
    },
    {
      "point": "Accountability versus simple answerability",
      "explanation": "Providing an explanation is only one part of accountability. A complete accountability process also requires investigation, appropriate response, correction, sanction or reinforcement, institutional learning and, where rights are harmed, an effective remedy."
    },
    {
      "point": "Accountability versus responsibility",
      "explanation": "Responsibility concerns who is assigned a task or duty. Accountability concerns how that person or institution demonstrates performance and legitimacy and accepts the consequences of exercise of authority."
    },
    {
      "point": "Accountability versus control",
      "explanation": "Control seeks to ensure that actors follow instructions or procedures. Accountability is broader because it asks whether decisions are lawful, evidence-based, responsive, in the public interest and capable of being justified to affected stakeholders."
    },
    {
      "point": "Accountability architecture",
      "explanation": "Public organizations are subject to political, legal, hierarchical, judicial, financial, social, professional and performance accountability. These systems should complement rather than replace one another."
    },
    {
      "point": "Accountability relationships",
      "explanation": "Accountability is relational: an official or agency is accountable to another body or public. Examples include an agency accountable to the legislature, a chief executive accountable to elected officials, and an administrator accountable to citizens and affected communities."
    },
    {
      "point": "Accountability mechanisms",
      "explanation": "Important mechanisms include access to information, public reporting, legislative hearings, internal control, external audit, judicial review, ombudsman institutions, complaint systems, procurement controls, performance evaluation, media scrutiny and citizen participation."
    },
    {
      "point": "Corrective and learning-oriented accountability",
      "explanation": "Consequences may include correction, compensation, disciplinary action, prosecution, organizational reform or recognition of good performance. Accountability should therefore include both enforcement and organizational learning."
    },
