      {
        "theory": "Public-choice theory",
        "application": "Ambiguous priority rules create opportunities for selective treatment, budget control, or informal benefits unless authority and decisions are transparent."
      },
      {
        "theory": "Principal-agent theory",
        "application": "If the mayor monitors only median processing time, the office may achieve speed by processing easy cases first while neglecting difficult or safety-sensitive cases."
      },
      {
        "theory": "Street-level bureaucracy",
        "application": "High caseloads, incomplete applications, and vague priority rules lead examiners to ration work, use informal criteria, and rely on professional judgment."
      },
      {
        "theory": "New Public Management",
        "application": "Service standards, case tracking, digital processing, and performance dashboards could improve speed and predictability, but they require safeguards against gaming."
      },
      {
        "theory": "New Public Service",
        "application": "Citizens, professional associations, and neighborhood groups should help clarify priorities, safety expectations, and appeal arrangements."
      },
      {
        "theory": "Digital governance",
        "application": "Automation can support document checks and status tracking, while human review, privacy protection, and offline access remain necessary."
      }
    ],
    "debrief_questions": [
      "Is the problem primarily caused by too much bureaucracy, too little bureaucracy, or poorly designed discretion?",
      "How can the office distinguish productive discretion from favoritism?",
