  "teaching_case": {
    "title": "The At-Risk Student Label in a Fictional University",
    "scenario": "Northbridge University creates an early-warning system based on attendance, assessment submission, and instructor reports. Students who cross a specified threshold are entered into an at-risk list. Advisors receive alerts, instructors may change the level of academic support offered, peers comment on the designation, and some students begin to describe themselves as at-risk. The university presents the category as an objective and evidence-based prediction of future academic difficulty. At the same time, first-year students from several disadvantaged backgrounds are disproportionately represented in the system, and some argue that missing classes is treated differently when it results from work, commuting, disability, or family responsibilities.",
    "learning_goal": "Demonstrate that a seemingly objective classification can be produced through institutional choices, embedded in routine practice, legitimized through claims of evidence, and made socially consequential.",
    "analysis_prompts": [
      "Which elements are observable events, and which are interpretations or classifications?",
      "Who participates in defining an at-risk student?",
      "Which formal and informal practices institutionalize the label?",
      "What reasons or evidence are used to legitimate the category?",
      "What resources, opportunities, identities, or behaviors may be changed by the label?",
      "Whose experiences or alternative definitions may be excluded?",
      "How could the label create a feedback loop between institutional response and student behavior?",
      "What would participant observation, interviews, document analysis, and discourse analysis each reveal?",
      "What are the ethical dangers of publishing students’ stories under this label?",
      "What safeguards would allow the study to examine the category without reinforcing stigma?"
    ],
    "expected_insights": [
      "The data used to create the category may be real while the threshold and interpretation remain socially and institutionally produced.",
      "A label can have material consequences even when the underlying problem is not imaginary.",
      "Official and participant meanings may differ, and these differences are analytically important.",
      "The study should examine prediction and the effects of prediction at the same time.",
      "A constructionist study does not eliminate the need for evidence; it changes what counts as relevant evidence and how categories are interrogated."
    ],
    "caution": "Do not imply that academic difficulties are merely labels. The case should be used to analyze how real inequalities and institutional procedures interact with socially authoritative categories."
  },
