      "Do not assume that all students recognize classical, biblical, or Western literary references. Provide context and assess interpretation rather than prior memorization.",
      "Make source cards optional supports rather than prerequisites for entering the discussion.",
      "Use read-aloud and visual mapping activities so that students have multiple ways to identify formal relationships.",
      "Avoid presenting Modernism as culturally uniform. Compare different historical locations, languages, genres, and experiences of modernity.",
      "When a Modernist writer draws on a non-Western tradition, examine the politics of selection, translation, publication, and representation.",
      "Emphasize that literary originality and intertextuality are not opposites; transformation is one way texts participate in cultural exchange."
    ],
    "common_misconceptions": [
      {
        "misconception": "Intertextuality means direct quotation.",
        "correction": "Quotation is only one type; implicit echoes, genres, structures, images, and ideas also create intertextual relations."
      },
      {
        "misconception": "A source explains the meaning of the later text.",
        "correction": "A source is part of a relationship, not a final key. Meaning emerges from the differences and contexts between texts."
      },
      {
        "misconception": "Intertextuality is simply plagiarism.",
        "correction": "Plagiarism presents another person's work without acknowledgment or transformation. Intertextual analysis asks how cultural texts are recontextualized."
      },
      {
        "misconception": "The reader must identify every source.",
        "correction": "Source recognition is useful but not always possible or necessary. Students should still analyze repetition, contrast, structure, genre, and unexplained familiarity."
      },
      {
        "misconception": "Modernist intertextuality always fragments meaning.",
        "correction": "It can fragment interpretation, but it can also create continuity, irony, authority, and cultural dialogue. Students should defend each function with evidence."
      }
    ]
  }
}

