        "explanation": "Because the groups no longer interact regularly, they exchange fewer genes. Each population begins evolving largely independently."
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "name": "Genetic divergence",
        "explanation": "Mutation, genetic drift, and natural selection produce different changes in the isolated populations. Different climates, resources, predators, and competitors may favor different adaptations."
      },
      {
        "step": 4,
        "name": "Development of reproductive barriers",
        "explanation": "Over many generations, differences in mating behavior, timing, body structure, habitat preference, or chromosome arrangement can prevent successful interbreeding. Even if the populations later reunite, they may no longer produce fertile offspring or may avoid mating with one another."
      },
