    "key_point": "Geographic isolation does not automatically produce a new species. It must be accompanied by sufficient genetic divergence and the development of reproductive isolation.",
    "summary": "In allopatric speciation, a physical barrier divides a population, gene flow declines, and the separated groups evolve differently. Mutations, genetic drift, and different selective pressures eventually produce reproductive isolation, resulting in one ancestral species becoming two or more species."
  }
}

