      },
      {
        "area": "Bounded rationality",
        "implication": "Because people have limited time, attention, information, and computational capacity, they rarely evaluate every option. Decisions are often 'good enough' rather than objectively optimal."
      },
      {
        "area": "Role of intuition",
        "implication": "Intuitive judgments can be useful when based on expertise and repeated experience, such as in firefighting or clinical diagnosis. However, intuition can be misleading when the situation is unfamiliar, emotionally charged, or based on misleading feedback."
      },
      {
        "area": "Cognitive load and decision quality",
        "implication": "Fatigue, stress, time pressure, information overload, and alcohol can weaken deliberative processing, allowing habitual or emotional responses to dominate behavior."
      },
      {
        "area": "Conflict between the two systems",
        "implication": "A person may consciously endorse one judgment while instinctively reacting differently. Such conflict can produce hesitation, rationalization, self-justification, or changes in behavior after the decision becomes difficult to reverse."
      },
      {
        "area": "Learning and expertise",
        "implication": "Deliberate practice can help patterns become automatic, explaining why experts sometimes respond rapidly and accurately. Expertise does not eliminate all biases, especially when experts encounter cases outside their domain."
      },
      {
        "area": "Emotional and motivational influences",
