      },
      {
        "area": "Environment and framing",
        "implication": "Decisions are influenced by how choices are presented, what information is visible, and whether the context encourages reflection. Defaults, labels, order effects, and available options can shape automatic choices."
      },
      {
        "area": "Behavior change and decision support",
        "implication": "Interventions can either strengthen deliberation through prompts, checklists, and feedback or support better automatic responses through defaults, training, and environmental design."
      },
      {
        "area": "Human-computer and AI interaction",
        "implication": "Automation bias may cause people to accept rapid algorithmic recommendations without adequate evaluation. Effective human-AI decision-making requires knowing when to trust intuition and when to verify outputs."
      },
      {
        "area": "Limits of the theory",
        "implication": "Dual process theory is a useful organizing framework, not a literal description of two separate brain systems. Research shows that reasoning is context-dependent, and not every judgment is adequately explained by simply labeling it as intuitive or analytical."
