      ]
    },
    {
      "focus": "Time, mortality, and existential anxiety",
      "interpretation": "The sea can symbolize the vastness of time and the human desire to achieve permanence or meaning. Mr Ramsay's philosophical reflections by the sea connect his thoughts to broader questions about human existence, death, and the limits of knowledge.",
      "evidence": [
        "Mr Ramsay's attempt to reach an abstract truth reflects the desire to impose a stable order on a changing world.",
        "His eventual recognition that human knowledge is partial can be linked to the sea's ability to overwhelm human perception."
      ]
    },
    {
      "focus": "Emotional and psychological depth",
      "interpretation": "The sea acts as a projection of the characters' inner lives. Calmness may suggest emotional stability, while storminess reflects conflict, grief, or unresolved feeling. Rather than functioning only as realistic scenery, it externalizes otherwise unspoken emotions.",
      "evidence": [
        "Lily Broughton's painting of the sea and the lighthouse demonstrates how external reality can be reshaped through perception and memory.",
        "Mrs Ramsay's absence in Part Two allows the sea to become a medium through which loss and the complexity of memory are explored."
      ]
    },
    {
      "focus": "Isolation and connection",
      "interpretation": "Although the sea separates characters from one another and from the lighthouse, it also links them to the same environment and emotional field. Its constantly changing surface illustrates the difference between apparent separation and underlying interconnectedness.",
      "evidence": [
        "The distance between the family and the lighthouse creates a sense of separation.",
        "The final journey toward the lighthouse temporarily brings characters together while the surrounding sea continues to signify change."
      ]
    },
    {
